  "publication_allowed": false,
  "rationale": "SHARED decodes every weight once instead of once per output row block and then pays an extra producer stage, a barrier, a global write of the expanded matrix and a stage-2 read of that matrix in every row block. On this target the paper shape is compute-bound: the hardware floor is about 1.16 ms for the same GEMM, and the whole REG kernel is only 4.8% above it, so even a perfect same-algorithm rewrite could recover only a single-digit percentage. Measured paired against the REG arm in the same run, the cheapest legal form (packed fp16 bit patterns in a poison fp32 scratch, no fill) is 2.59% slower at paper and 7.94x slower at small; the fp32 poison form is 9.2% slower, and the fp16 device_zero form is 47% slower because the fill is a timed cost. The no-fill measurement on identical kernels is still slower than REG, so the fill is not the reason. Therefore no SHARED candidate was written and REG is kept.",
  "registered_baseline_gate": "the repository's fresh performance gate is latency_ratio = candidate_us / baseline_us <= 1.10 against the registered baseline (tcenv/portfolio_performance/performance.py, tcenv/portfolio_performance/performance.py::MAX_REGRESSION_RATIO). It is recorded here only for context: at paper the cheapest legal SHARED estimate would pass it (about 0.97x of the fastest baseline), so that gate is NOT the reason SHARED was rejected; the rejection rests on the measured comparison against REG.",
